## 9. IMAGE QUALITY & DISPLAY RULES

- Fixed aspect-ratio containers for every image (poster `2/3` or `5/7`, banner `16/9` or hero crop, episode thumbs `16/9`, avatars `1/1`). Use `object-fit: cover`; posters `object-position: center top` (faces/titles near the top); banners `center 30%` unless focal data exists.
- Request the **right size per slot** (e.g. rail posters ~2× display width for retina; hero banner up to ~1920w; detail poster ~400w). Prefer the largest legitimate source (AniList `extraLarge`/`large` cover, `bannerImage`) where the slot needs it, and `medium` for tiny thumbs (Top 10 rows, characters).
- Never upscale low-res images and claim improved quality; if only a small source exists, show it at its natural scale inside the container with a tasteful fallback (blurred cover background) rather than stretching.
- Provide placeholders: dominant-colour or `#111` background while loading, and a neutral fallback tile with the purple "NO IMAGE" treatment (as seen for characters) on error. No broken-image icons.
- Avoid heavy re-compression: keep JPEG/WebP quality ~80–85, AVIF ~60–70.
- Always set `width`/`height` (or aspect-ratio) to prevent layout shift.
- Use `decoding="async"`; lazy-load all below-the-fold images; **the hero image and the first row of posters visible above the fold are eager** (hero gets `fetchpriority="high"`).
- Consistent hairline border and radius on all image containers.

